WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.674
Madeleine Albright was a refugee twice before

2
00:00:02.674 --> 00:00:05.153
she was twelve — from Hitler, then from Stalin.

3
00:00:05.503 --> 00:00:10.205
The family arrived in New York on 11 November 1948.

4
00:00:10.555 --> 00:00:11.426
She was eleven.

5
00:00:11.776 --> 00:00:12.542
She remembered:

6
00:00:13.142 --> 00:00:16.273
“We steamed into New York Harbor, past the Statue of

7
00:00:16.273 --> 00:00:19.644
Liberty, with tears in our eyes and hope in our hearts.”

8
00:00:20.244 --> 00:00:23.358
When Nazi Germany occupied Czechoslovakia in 1939,

9
00:00:23.358 --> 00:00:26.472
the family fled to London, where she spent the war

10
00:00:26.472 --> 00:00:29.648
years as a small child in a city under bombardment.

11
00:00:29.998 --> 00:00:31.368
They went home after the war.

12
00:00:31.718 --> 00:00:35.089
In February 1948 the Communists seized power,

13
00:00:35.089 --> 00:00:38.684
and the Korbels left again — this time for good.

14
00:00:39.034 --> 00:00:41.472
The girl from Prague started calling herself Madeleine.

15
00:00:41.822 --> 00:00:45.560
She became a United States citizen in 1957, and graduated from

16
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.600
Wellesley College with honors in political science two years later.

17
00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.246
On 23 January 1997 she was sworn in as the

18
00:00:53.246 --> 00:00:58.426
sixty-fourth Secretary of State — the first woman to hold the job.

19
00:00:58.776 --> 00:01:01.701
Because she was born abroad, she could never be President.

20
00:01:02.051 --> 00:01:06.019
On 10 November 1998 — fifty years, less one day, after

21
00:01:06.019 --> 00:01:09.987
she arrived — she and her sister renewed their oath of

22
00:01:09.987 --> 00:01:14.323
allegiance at a naturalization ceremony in Washington, D.C.

23
00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:16.020
She told the new citizens:

24
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.245
“Today marks a new beginning in your lives, and an ongoing chapter in

25
00:01:20.245 --> 00:01:23.922
the story of America which is, above all else, a story of immigrants.”

26
00:01:24.522 --> 00:01:29.730
On 12 March 1999, at the Truman Library in Independence, Missouri, she received

27
00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:34.937
the documents that made the Czech Republic, Hungary and Poland members of NATO.

28
00:01:35.286 --> 00:01:37.380
The country she had fled as a child was now

29
00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.571
an ally of the country that had taken her in.

30
00:01:39.921 --> 00:01:42.377
If you moved to a new country, what would help you

31
00:01:42.377 --> 00:01:45.226
feel that you belonged there, and did not just live there?
